The D-row assertions in test_stats_derivation_congested:
```
	for d in rec.drops {
		testing.expect(t, d.reason == u8(Drop_Reason.Queue_Overflow), "D rows are E9 only (E22 excluded)")
		testing.expect(t, d.count >= 1, "D-row counts are >= 1")
		found := false
		for p in rec.pipes {
			if p.pipe == d.pipe {
				found = true
				break
			}
		}
		testing.expect(t, found, "every D row names a live pipe")
	}
	// per-pipe: dropped column == sum of its D-row counts (the replication)
	for p in rec.pipes {
		sum_d := u32(0)
		for d in rec.drops {
			if d.pipe == p.pipe {
				sum_d += d.count
			}
		}
		testing.expect_value(t, p.dropped, sum_d)
	}
```
No assertion checks triple uniqueness or an exact count. Probe of the tick-12 record shows the scenario does exercise aggregation (`D pipe=1 class=1 reason=0 count=2` — two same-kind sheds in one tick), but if stats_compute's find-or-append (core/stats.odin 'if d.pipe == pipe && d.class == site.class && d.reason == u8(site.reason) { d.count += 1 ... }') regressed to append-per-shed (two rows of count 1), sum_d stays 2==2 and every assertion passes.

